    "body": "Concave grout printed as `\u00bf`, the same marker as convex grout, so persisted text was ambiguous \u2014 and a text-persisted document with an infix hole hit the slow recovering parser on every reload, because `1 \u00bf 2` lexes as three operands.\n\nConcave grout now prints as `\u29d6` (U+29D6) and round-trips through the fast path: lexer token, identifier-class carve-out (same pattern as `\u00bf`), and a `BinHole` production in every sort the editor molds it for \u2014 Exp, Pat (including fun parameters), Typ (including fun-parameter ascriptions) and TPat.\n\nPrecedence follows the editor's `Precedence.concave_grout`: looser than every binary operator, tighter than `;` and the let/fun/if/case bodies, so `let x = 1 in x \u29d6 2` keeps the hole in the body and `(Int -> Bool) \u29d6 String` reduces the arrow first. To express the latter with one token, the arrow-bodied binders (`fun`/`fix`/`typfun`, `poly`/`rec`) take their own `BINDER_BODY` level instead of the arrow token's; their behaviour is otherwise unchanged (the existing Menhir/fuzz/corpus suites pass as before).\n\nConversion flattens a parsed chain into one flat `MultiHole` and prints a uniform-sort `MultiHole` back as a chain; mixed-sort holes have no textual spelling and stay on the slow path.\n\nBase-type keywords (`Int`, `Bool`, `String`, \u2026) are now constructor patterns in Menhir as they already were in exp position: deleting the arrow in `let x : Int -> Bool` leaves a pattern-level hole beside `Bool`, and that document (and plain `let Bool = 1`) used to fall to the recovering parser on every load.\n\nGates: `TextRoundtrip.EditedGrout` \u2014 12 programs where grout is left by simulated typing (not hand-written markers) in every sort: the persisted text must fast-parse, keep its grout through the load-time regrout, and reprint as a fixed point. `MenhirParser.ConcaveMarker` \u2014 33 cases pinning Menhir \u2261 MakeTerm through the marker load path (`MarkerParse.of_text`, where the marker becomes real grout) across all four sorts \u2014 and `TextRoundtrip.ConcaveMarker` (14 fast-parse/fixed-point cases incl. legacy `\u00bf` canonicalization). One divergence class is deliberately not pinned: the sort tag MakeTerm gives a hole's neighbour (`let x : Int \u29d6 y` sorts `y` as a type, Menhir as a pattern \u2014 same structure, same tokens).\n\nGrammar conflicts: +1 s/r state and +1 r/r state over dev, both in the documented families (UNIT lookahead after `pat \u29d6 pat`; funAscAtom sharing nonAscriptingPat), listed in the grammar's KNOWN CONFLICT FAMILIES comment.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
